INT. LAKE CABIN, KITCHEN - DAY

A pine-panelled kitchen that smells of woodsmoke and dust. Cardboard boxes stack on a table where somebody's father once ate breakfast. Through the window a grey lake lies flat as a plate.

MARGUERITE LAVOIE, fifty-two, in a cardigan, wraps a chipped teacup in newspaper. Her sister ODILE LAVOIE, forty-eight, in a denim jacket, stands at the open refrigerator with a bin bag, holding a jar of pickles at arm's length. At the counter DESMOND OYELARAN, Marguerite's husband, taps at a calculator. By the screen door a neighbour, MR. ALBRECHT, seventy, in rubber boots, holds a fishing rod he has not been asked to hold.

MARGUERITE
That was Maman's cup.

ODILE
It's chipped.

MARGUERITE
It was chipped when she bought it.

She sets the cup in the box. She does not look at her sister. She picks up the next cup.

ODILE
He kept pickles from 2009.

MARGUERITE
He kept everything.

ODILE
That's not a virtue.

She drops the jar in the bin bag. The glass thuds, muffled, and does not break. Both of them listen for it to break. Neither says so.

DESMOND
The realtor wants to list by the fifteenth.

MARGUERITE
The fifteenth is a Sunday.

DESMOND
Then the sixteenth.

ODILE
(to the bin bag)
She'll list when she's ready.

MARGUERITE
Who's she?

ODILE
You. She. I don't know.

She ties the bag. She carries it past Mr. Albrecht, who steps aside, then follows with the rod.

MR. ALBRECHT
Your father lent me this in '94.

MARGUERITE
Keep it.

MR. ALBRECHT
It isn't mine to keep.

ODILE
(from the porch, not turning)
He'd want you to have it.

She sets the bag at the curb. The screen door bangs behind her. Marguerite stands very still at the table, her hand flat on a cardboard lid.

DESMOND
Marguerite.

MARGUERITE
I'm fine.

DESMOND
I know.

He crosses the kitchen and unfolds her fingers from the lid. He takes the cup out of the box, unwraps it, fills it from the kettle, and puts it in her hands.

MR. ALBRECHT
(clearing his throat)
I'll leave the rod on the porch, then.

He goes. The kitchen is quiet.

Through the window Odile has sat down on the dock, her back to the cabin, her boots dangling above the water. Marguerite watches her through the glass. She lifts the cup, drinks, and does not set it down.

After a moment she carries the cup out the screen door and across the grass. She lowers herself onto the planks beside her sister. She holds the cup out without a word.

Odile takes it. She drinks. She hands it back.

The lake does not move.
